# `pricing::HestonFftQuadrature`

The weights of the integral inside the FFT pricing: Simpson or trapezoidal.

The Carr-Madan transform approximates an integral over the integration variable by a weighted sum. Simpson weights are more accurate for smooth integrands at the same step; trapezoidal weights are simpler and can be preferable when the integrand oscillates quickly.

## Fields

### `Simpson`

`enumerator`, default `0`.

Simpson weights. The default.

### `Trapezoidal`

`enumerator`, default `1`.

Trapezoidal weights.

## Guidance

Set as `Fft::quadrature`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The two rules agree to many decimals at the default step.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const auto inst = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0);
  const auto mkt = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0);
  const auto mdl = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}).as<fin::model::Heston>();

  for (const auto q : {fin::pricing::HestonFftQuadrature::Simpson, fin::pricing::HestonFftQuadrature::Trapezoidal}) {
    fin::pricer::Fft e;
    e.quadrature = q;
    out.writeln(static_cast<int>(q), ":", e.price(inst, mdl, mkt).price.items<double>()[0]);
  }
}
```

### Example 2

At a coarse step the trapezoidal rule is the rougher.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto inst = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0);
  const auto mkt = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0);
  const auto mdl = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}).as<fin::model::Heston>();

  for (const auto q : {fin::pricing::HestonFftQuadrature::Simpson, fin::pricing::HestonFftQuadrature::Trapezoidal}) {
    fin::pricer::Fft e;
    e.quadrature = q;
    e.du = 0.6;
    out.writeln(static_cast<int>(q), ":", e.price(inst, mdl, mkt).price.items<double>()[0], "(COS 8.62811)");
  }
}
```

### Example 3

Through a Field.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Fft, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};

  field.controls.fft.quadrature = fin::pricing::HestonFftQuadrature::Trapezoidal;
  out.writeln(field.price().price.items<double>()[0]);
}
```

